ICCV-2025-P2497,ICCV-2025-A014821,Yuxuan Luo,shared,3,5,5,"Wangxuan Institute of Computer Technology, Peking University Xi’an Jiaotong University What’s the style of this artwork? What’s the content in the red bounding box? What’s the content of this calligraphy? The calligraphy style in this work is caoshu ( 草书 ), or Cursive Script. This style is known for its fast and fluid brushstrokes, where the characters are written in a highly simplified and abbreviated form. 行人使长安可怜 Who’s the author of this artwork? Shen Peng, a prominent contemporary Chinese calligrapher known for his distinctive and powerful artistic style. In what occasion might the author create this calligraphy? The occasion for creating this calligraphy could be a personal celebration or a gift for someone named ""Kin Hai."" The calligraphy features a poem by the Song Dynasty poet Xin Qiji, which expresses a sense of longing and nostalgia for the past. This piece was created with care and intention, possibly for a special event or as a token of appreciation. What’s the layout of this artwork? 郁孤台下清江水中间多少行人泪西北望 长安可怜无数山青山遮不住毕竟东流去 江晚正愁余山深闻鹧鸪辛稼轩词三首为 京海同志沈鹏书 The layout is shoujuan ( 手卷 ), or handscroll, a traditional format often used in Chinese calligraphy. The text is arranged horizontally, with the characters flowing from right to left. (a) Content recognition and interpretation of a Chinese calligraphy artwork in a contextualized interaction. How many attractions are on this sign? What is written on these red envelopes? There are four attractions on this sign: Wofo Temple, Cherry Valley, Lilac Garden, and Peony Garden. 龙凤呈祥琴瑟和鸣 鸳鸯比翼白首齐眉 Which attraction is closest to me? The closest attraction to you is Lilac Garden. (Single-turn recognition) √:150m (b) Performance comparison with other VLMs. How much further do I need to walk to get to Cherry Valley? You need to walk another meters to reach Cherry Valley. √: Cherry Valley (1150m) (Multi-turn recognition and comprehension) (c) Recognition and comprehension in real-world scenes. Figure . We propose CalliReader, a versatile vision-language model (VLM) for Chinese Calligraphy Contextualization (CC2 ). It excels in full-page/region-wise recognition, multilingual interpretation, and intent identification of calligraphy artworks (a), outperforming finetuned state-of-the-art VLMs (b), while also demonstrating applicability in general scene-text recognition and comprehension (c).",-5,data/raw/iccv/2025/first_pages/ICCV-2025-P2497.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2025/papers/Luo_CalliReader_Contextualizing_Chinese_Calligraphy_via_an_Embedding-Aligned_Vision-Language_Model_ICCV_2025_paper.pdf
