ICCV-2025-P2519,ICCV-2025-A014937,Tianyuan Qu,shared,3,6,6,"CUHK HKUST MIN : : : : Global sparse uniform sampling VLM Question (Low Necessary Sampling Density): What is the journey of the camera wearer in the video? A. Took a bus to a restaurant for a meal, then walk to a nearby supermarket for groceries, and finally took a bus back home. B. Took a bus to a cafe for a drink, then took a bus to a bakery to buy some bread, and finally took a bus back home. C. Took a bus to a restaurant for a meal, then took a bus to a supermarket for groceries, and finally took a bus back home. D. Took a bus to a supermarket for groceries, then took a bus to a restaurant for a meal, and finally took a bus back home. : : Too few visual cues! Global dense uniform sampling At least key frame per minute to answer VLM : : SEC Too much redundancy! Too slow! Question (High Necessary Sampling Density): What is the sequence of the camera wearer's actions when bagging items at the supermarket checkout before paying with their phone? A. First bag the chicken wings, followed by the beef, minced meat, and finally the potatoes and ham. B. First bag the ham and potatoes, followed by the chicken wings, minced meat, and finally the beef. C. First bag the minced meat, followed by the ham, potatoes, beef, and finally the chicken wings. D. First bag the chicken wings, followed by the beef, potatoes, and finally the yogurt and banana. Sampling At least key frame per second to answer Q1 Q2 Computation Cost Global Sparse Frame / min Frames Global Dense Frame / sec Frames Local Dense Frame / sec Frames Figure . Illustration of the Sampling Dilemma in Long Video Analysis: (Left) In Q1, identifying a camera wearer’s visited locations requires analyzing the entire video. However, key frames are sparse, so sampling one frame per minute often provides enough information. In contrast, Q2 examines the packing order during checkout, requiring high-resolution sampling to capture rapid actions. (Right) Sampling Dilemma emerges in tasks like Q2: a low sampling density fails to provide sufficient visual cues for accurate answers, while a high sampling density results in redundant frames, significantly slowing inference speed. This challenge underscores the need for adaptive sampling strategies, especially for tasks with high necessary sampling density.",-4,data/raw/iccv/2025/first_pages/ICCV-2025-P2519.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2025/papers/Qu_Does_Your_Vision-Language_Model_Get_Lost_in_the_Long_Video_ICCV_2025_paper.pdf
