ICCV-2025-P2649,ICCV-2025-A015703,Robin Swanson,shared,3,5,5,"University of Toronto Dunlap Institute for Astronomy & Astrophysics International Gemini Observatory University of Victoria N Jointly Optimized Phases Telescope and Learned Mirror Phases N Sub-Exposures Under-Sampled Images with Induced Sub-Pixel Shis Jointly Optimized Super Resolution Neural Network Super-Resolved Image Figure . Super Resolved Imaging with Adaptive Optics is a computational imaging method to enable multi-image super-resolution in scientific telescopes. Without any additional hardware, existing adaptive mirrors are used to inject diversity into each sub-exposure. When jointly reconstructed, we are able to recover higher spatial resolution from an otherwise undersampled signal. Each exposure’s phase profiles is jointly optimized along with the image reconstruction algorithm—resulting in greatly improved performance.",-7,data/raw/iccv/2025/first_pages/ICCV-2025-P2649.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2025/papers/Swanson_Super_Resolved_Imaging_with_Adaptive_Optics_ICCV_2025_paper.pdf
